-- allium: 3
-- stale-tool-results.allium
--
-- Stale Tool Result Clearing: Behavioural Specification
--
-- Scope: The retention pass applied while a conversation's persisted
--        history is folded into the provider-agnostic LLM message list
--        in Executor::build_llm_messages_static. The pass maintains a
--        monotonic per-conversation clear watermark and, from it, decides
--        per tool-result message whether to send the result body
--        verbatim or replace it with a placeholder — covering the
--        result's text and images together — to save context-window
--        space and input tokens on long sessions while keeping the
--        cleared request prefix stable across turns for cache reuse.
--
-- Includes: The clear watermark and its monotonic, pressure-gated,
--           worthwhile-gain-gated advancement; the recency floor the
--           watermark may not cross; the per-result verdict derived from
--           watermark and tool clearability; the unified text+image
--           treatment; and the invariants that make clearing recoverable,
--           cache-stable, and non-destructive.
--
-- Excludes: Token estimation internals (estimate_text_tokens is treated
--           as an opaque input). Provider wire translation and cache
--           breakpoint placement (see specs/llm/anthropic.allium; this
--           spec only guarantees the recency floor keeps the trailing
--           result — the block the last-user-message breakpoint anchors
--           on — uncleared). Continuation/summarization, the heavier
--           retention tier described in design.md.
--
-- Normative companions:
--   specs/stale-tool-results/requirements.md — REQ-STR-001..011
--   specs/stale-tool-results/design.md       — watermark model, caching
--
-- Cross-references:
--   crates/phoenix-ide/src/runtime/executor.rs — build_llm_messages_static,
--       flatten_tool_blocks, cap_messages_to_token_budget,
--       estimate_text_tokens
--   crates/phoenix-tools/src/lib.rs            — Tool::clearable, ToolOutput
--
-- Self-contained: this spec does not `use` any other module. The
-- persisted source of truth (db::ToolContent), the assembled
-- llm/LlmMessage list, and the producing tool are treated opaquely; only
-- the facts the retention decision branches on are modelled. The
-- watermark's persistence across turns (the new_watermark of one
-- assembly is the prior_watermark of the next) is a temporal fact noted
-- in prose, not expressible in this dialect's entity-snapshot
-- quantification.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Enumerations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The decision for one tool-result message in one assembled request.
-- `kept` sends the persisted result body and images verbatim; `cleared`
-- replaces the body with a fixed placeholder and sends no images. The
-- paired tool-call block is always retained regardless of verdict, so a
-- cleared result is never a silent gap (REQ-STR-004).
enum RetentionVerdict { kept | cleared }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One tool-result message as seen by the retention pass. Derived from a
-- persisted db::ToolContent joined to its producing tool-call by
-- tool_use_id; the pass never mutates the persisted source.
--
-- `round_sequence_id` is the message sequence position of the result's
-- tool round — the same monotonic space the watermark lives in. A result
-- is within the watermark's reach iff its round_sequence_id is at or
-- below the watermark.
--
-- `within_recency_floor` is true iff the result is in the most recent
-- keep_recent_rounds rounds. The watermark may never reach these
-- (WatermarkBehindRecencyFloor), so a floored result is always kept;
-- modelling it explicitly lets the trailing-result guarantee be stated
-- directly (REQ-STR-003, REQ-STR-007).
--
-- `producing_tool_clearable` is the Tool::clearable() capability of the
-- tool that produced the result: true iff re-invoking the tool would
-- reproduce the information (REQ-STR-002). Defaulted false for any tool
-- that has not opted in.
--
-- `token_estimate` is estimate_text_tokens over the result body (and its
-- images) — the tokens this result contributes, and the tokens freed if
-- it is cleared.
--
-- `images_sent` records whether image blocks are emitted for this result.
-- It is coupled to the verdict (REQ-STR-011): kept results send images,
-- cleared results do not — there is no separate image schedule.
value ToolResultRendering {
    tool_use_id: String
    round_sequence_id: Integer
    within_recency_floor: Boolean
    producing_tool_clearable: Boolean
    token_estimate: Integer
    verdict: RetentionVerdict
    images_sent: Boolean
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- One invocation of the history-assembly retention pass: the facts about
-- a single request being built that the pass decides over.
--
-- `prior_watermark` is the conversation's persisted clear watermark as of
-- this assembly; `new_watermark` is its value after this assembly's
-- (possible) advance, persisted back to the conversation. Across turns,
-- one assembly's new_watermark is the next assembly's prior_watermark.
--
-- `clear_trigger_tokens` is the high-water mark above which a sweep is
-- considered (REQ-STR-001); `target_after_clear_tokens` is the usage a
-- sweep aims to fall back under. Both are derived upstream from the
-- model's context window and deployment config (REQ-STR-010), so only the
-- resulting integer thresholds appear here.
--
-- `floor_sequence_id` is the highest round_sequence_id the watermark may
-- reach: the boundary just before the most recent keep_recent_rounds
-- rounds. The watermark is capped at it (REQ-STR-003).
--
-- `freed_tokens` is the tokens this assembly's sweep frees: the sum of
-- token_estimate over results newly cleared by advancing the watermark.
-- Zero on a turn that does not advance.
entity HistoryAssembly {
    estimated_input_tokens: Integer
    clear_trigger_tokens: Integer
    target_after_clear_tokens: Integer
    floor_sequence_id: Integer
    prior_watermark: Integer
    new_watermark: Integer
    freed_tokens: Integer
    renderings: List<ToolResultRendering>
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- Tool rounds whose results are always retained verbatim, counted
    -- from the most recent round. Covers the agent's active working set
    -- (REQ-STR-003) and always includes the trailing result that anchors
    -- the last cache breakpoint (REQ-STR-007). The watermark may not
    -- reach these rounds. Deployment-tunable (REQ-STR-010).
    keep_recent_rounds: Integer = 3

    -- Minimum tokens a sweep must free, or the watermark holds this turn
    -- (REQ-STR-006). Bounds how often a sweep disturbs the cached prefix;
    -- sized so the one-time cache-write cost is recovered by the ongoing
    -- savings. Deployment-tunable (REQ-STR-010).
    clear_at_least_tokens: Integer = 8192
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

-- The history-assembly pass itself, one invocation per request built.
-- Anchors HistoryAssembly to a concrete runtime consumer; rules below do
-- not reference it by name.
actor HistoryAssembler {
    identified_by: HistoryAssembly
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — watermark advancement
------------------------------------------------------------

-- Below the high-water mark the transcript fits comfortably: the
-- watermark holds and nothing new is cleared this turn (REQ-STR-001).
-- Within a conversation input usage only grows, so a held watermark below
-- the trigger is a one-way condition until usage climbs past it.
rule WatermarkHeldBelowPressure {
    when: HistoryAssembled(assembly)

    requires: assembly.estimated_input_tokens <= assembly.clear_trigger_tokens

    ensures: assembly.new_watermark = assembly.prior_watermark
    ensures: assembly.freed_tokens = 0
}

-- Over the high-water mark, but advancing the watermark to the next round
-- boundary that would help frees less than clear_at_least: the watermark
-- holds rather than pay a cache invalidation too small to be worth it
-- (REQ-STR-006). It waits for more clearable output to accumulate.
rule WatermarkHeldInsufficientGain {
    when: HistoryAssembled(assembly)

    requires: assembly.estimated_input_tokens > assembly.clear_trigger_tokens
    requires: assembly.freed_tokens < clear_at_least_tokens

    ensures: assembly.new_watermark = assembly.prior_watermark
    ensures: assembly.freed_tokens = 0
}

-- Over the high-water mark with a worthwhile sweep available: the
-- watermark advances forward over whole rounds, never past the recency
-- floor, freeing at least clear_at_least tokens. It moves far enough that
-- usage falls under the target (so the next sweep is many turns away), or
-- to the floor if the target cannot be reached without crossing it.
rule WatermarkAdvancedUnderPressure {
    when: HistoryAssembled(assembly)

    requires: assembly.estimated_input_tokens > assembly.clear_trigger_tokens
    requires: assembly.freed_tokens >= clear_at_least_tokens

    ensures: assembly.new_watermark > assembly.prior_watermark
    ensures: assembly.new_watermark <= assembly.floor_sequence_id
    ensures: assembly.freed_tokens >= clear_at_least_tokens

    @guidance
        -- Advance new_watermark to the highest round boundary R such that
        -- R <= floor_sequence_id and clearing the clearable results in
        -- (prior_watermark, R] brings estimated usage to <=
        -- target_after_clear_tokens; if no such R reaches the target,
        -- advance to floor_sequence_id. Advancement is by whole rounds so
        -- a tool_use/tool_result pairing is never split. freed_tokens is
        -- the sum of token_estimate over results in (prior_watermark,
        -- new_watermark] whose producing tool is clearable. The watermark
        -- is persisted back to the conversation row; next turn reads it as
        -- prior_watermark.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules — per-result verdict (derived from the watermark)
------------------------------------------------------------

-- A result is cleared iff it is within the (post-advance) watermark's
-- reach AND its producing tool is clearable. Clearing drops the body and
-- the images together (REQ-STR-002, REQ-STR-011).
rule ToolResultCleared {
    when: RenderingDecided(assembly, r)

    requires: r.round_sequence_id <= assembly.new_watermark
    requires: r.producing_tool_clearable = true

    ensures: r.verdict = cleared
    ensures: r.images_sent = false

    @guidance
        -- Clearing replaces the rendered ToolResult body with the fixed
        -- placeholder "[tool result cleared to save context]" and emits no
        -- image blocks; the paired assistant ToolUse block is left intact
        -- (REQ-STR-004). The persisted db::ToolContent is read-only here —
        -- the cleared form exists only in the Vec<LlmMessage> for this one
        -- request (REQ-STR-005).
}

-- A result beyond the watermark's reach is kept in full: it is recent
-- enough that the watermark has not reached it (and, within the recency
-- floor, never will). Text and images both sent (REQ-STR-003, REQ-STR-011).
rule ToolResultKeptBeyondWatermark {
    when: RenderingDecided(assembly, r)

    requires: r.round_sequence_id > assembly.new_watermark

    ensures: r.verdict = kept
    ensures: r.images_sent = true
}

-- A result within the watermark's reach but whose producing tool is not
-- clearable is kept: its information could not be regenerated by
-- re-invocation, so removal would be unrecoverable data loss. The
-- watermark passing it by does not clear it (REQ-STR-002).
rule ToolResultKeptNotClearable {
    when: RenderingDecided(assembly, r)

    requires: r.round_sequence_id <= assembly.new_watermark
    requires: r.producing_tool_clearable = false

    ensures: r.verdict = kept
    ensures: r.images_sent = true
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

-- REQ-STR-007. The watermark never regresses within an assembly: every
-- advancement rule leaves new_watermark >= prior_watermark. Combined with
-- the temporal carry (one turn's new_watermark is the next's
-- prior_watermark), the cleared set only ever grows — the basis for a
-- byte-stable cleared prefix across turns and the cache reuse it buys.
invariant WatermarkMonotonic {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        a.new_watermark >= a.prior_watermark
}

-- REQ-STR-003 / REQ-STR-007. The watermark stays strictly behind the
-- recency floor, so the most recent keep_recent_rounds rounds — including
-- the trailing tool result the last-user-message cache breakpoint anchors
-- on (specs/llm/anthropic.allium) — are never within its reach.
invariant WatermarkBehindRecencyFloor {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        a.new_watermark <= a.floor_sequence_id
}

-- REQ-STR-003. A result in the recency floor is always kept, whatever the
-- watermark and whatever its producing tool. Its round sits above the
-- floor boundary, hence above the watermark.
invariant RecencyFloorAlwaysKept {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        for r in a.renderings:
            r.within_recency_floor = true implies r.verdict = kept
}

-- REQ-STR-002. A cleared result's producing tool is always clearable:
-- unrecoverable output is never removed.
invariant ClearedImpliesRecoverable {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        for r in a.renderings:
            r.verdict = cleared implies r.producing_tool_clearable = true
}

-- REQ-STR-007. A cleared result is always within the watermark's reach.
-- Since the watermark only advances (WatermarkMonotonic) and a floored
-- result is never within reach (WatermarkBehindRecencyFloor), a result
-- cleared on one turn stays within reach and stays cleared on every later
-- turn — the structural witness for cross-turn stability, which the
-- temporal carry of the watermark completes.
invariant ClearedImpliesWithinWatermark {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        for r in a.renderings:
            r.verdict = cleared implies r.round_sequence_id <= a.new_watermark
}

-- REQ-STR-011. Image emission is governed by the same verdict as the
-- body: a kept result sends its images, a cleared result does not. There
-- is no separate image-retirement schedule.
invariant ImagesFollowVerdict {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        for r in a.renderings:
            r.images_sent = (r.verdict = kept)
}

-- REQ-STR-006. When a sweep advances the watermark it frees at least the
-- worthwhile-gain minimum; a smaller sweep is not taken (the watermark
-- holds with freed_tokens = 0).
invariant SweepFreesWorthwhileOrHolds {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        a.freed_tokens > 0 implies a.freed_tokens >= clear_at_least_tokens
}

-- REQ-STR-008. The freed-token figure the pass logs equals the
-- contribution of the results newly cleared by this turn's advance,
-- never an estimate detached from the watermark move.
invariant FreedTokensMatchNewlyCleared {
    for a in HistoryAssemblies:
        a.freed_tokens = sum_token_estimate_newly_cleared(a)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Design Decisions
------------------------------------------------------------
-- Kept as prose rather than `open question` declarations so the file
-- remains parseable.
--
-- A persisted monotonic watermark, not a stateless positional rule.
--     Cache reuse holds only while the removed prefix is byte-identical
--     turn to turn. A tail-relative rule ("keep the last N rounds") moves
--     its boundary every turn and rewrites recent context every turn; a
--     threshold-only rule un-clears what a sweep just cleared once usage
--     drops back under the mark. Stability therefore requires remembering
--     how far clearing has reached. The watermark is that memory — one
--     monotonic integer (a message sequence_id) on the conversation row,
--     a scalar column rather than a blob field, advanced only by a sweep
--     and never regressed.
--
-- One mechanism for text and images. Image emission is coupled to the
--     verdict (ImagesFollowVerdict), replacing the prior image-only
--     retirement window. That window retired images a fixed two rounds
--     behind the newest round — a tail-relative boundary that advanced
--     every turn and rewrote recent context every turn, invalidating the
--     cache from two rounds behind the tail through the tail. Folding
--     images into the watermark-governed verdict removes that per-turn
--     rewrite: images leave the context only when their round is swept,
--     at a boundary that holds still between sweeps (REQ-STR-011).
--
-- Recoverability is a tool capability, not a content heuristic. Clearing
--     keys on Tool::clearable() — a property of the producing tool
--     resolved via tool_use_id — not on inspecting the result body. A
--     false default means a newly added tool is never silently cleared;
--     opting in is an explicit, reviewed act. The watermark reaching an
--     unclearable result does not clear it (REQ-STR-002).
--
-- One-time invalidation per sweep, then re-warm. Advancing the watermark
--     rewrites the prefix from the earliest newly-cleared result forward,
--     invalidating the cache there. This is inherent to any in-place
--     context reduction. The design pays it once per sweep (gated by
--     clear_at_least_tokens and spaced by target_after_clear) and relies
--     on the watermark holding between sweeps to re-warm: every
--     intervening turn sends the same cleared prefix and reads it from
--     cache.
--
-- Non-destructive by construction. The only persisted state added is the
--     watermark integer; the cleared body is recomputed from it on every
--     assembly and never stored. A UI rebuilding from storage, or a
--     rewind, sees every tool result in full (REQ-STR-005).
